  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84195,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84195.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Sure. Well, one thing that's important to understand is that elections administration and elections laws really begin and belong predominantly with the individual states. That's what our Constitution directs us to do, and we certainly respect those principles of federalism and Congress. So, you know, we are going to let states make decisions about the types of policies that they think are appropriate. And in Congress, our role is that we can work together in partnership with each of the states to help identify which of those practices have been successful. So that's why we began where we did today. We really took a look across the country at places that had been successful. And it hoped to really walk through the laws, also the pieces of administration and logistics And I'll tell you, a third piece that's really important when it comes to successful elections and elections integrity is cybersecurity and infrastructure. So one of my objectives as chair of this committee is to take a look at the things that we all share, the concerns that we all share that are universal across the country. And those concerns include things like emergency preparation, being ready for severe weather events, cybersecurity being assured that our cyber and digital infrastructure is prepared to meet any threats that it might face. And then also the physical infrastructure and voting systems and equipment, ensuring that we have standards for that type of thing and that we're helping share out those best practices and resources with the states so that they have the tools they need to be successful.",
